// The marbles — soup.ron, seeded with a lineage that has paid to stay round.
//
// This file exists so the two pictures can be put side by side without any tool use. Open
// `soup.ron` and you get a tessellation: cells flattened into polygons, sharing walls, no gaps
// anywhere. Open this and you get a heap of separate round bodies. Nothing about the world
// differs between them — the chemistry, the light, the size and the seeding are `soup.ron`'s,
// copied — and nothing about the *simulation* differs either. Only the genome does.
//
//   mm-cli run scenarios/the_marbles.ron --ticks 20000 --check
//
// The mechanism is in `docs/STIFFNESS.md` and the arithmetic is in `genomes/marble.mm`'s own
// header. In one line: a cell bulges into the space its neighbours leave unless it is either
// glued to them or too firm to bulge, and `marble.mm` is neither glued nor soft.
//
// Measured, on this slide against the same slide seeded with `ancestor.mm`:
//
//   genome         population   rigidity   swell   coverage   radius
//   ancestor.mm         1 113       0.09    1.229       105%     1.12
//   marble.mm             666       0.68    1.095       105%     1.38
//
// where 1.000 is a perfect sphere cut by its seams and 1.237 is a cell inflated until its
// clipped outline keeps the area it has. **Four cells in ten** is what the picture costs, and it
// is charged every tick forever rather than once.
//
// Coverage is the number that decides whether this looks like anything. A rounder strain is
// available — a vacuole and a wall at the catalogue's maximum reaches a swell of 1.015 — and it
// settles at 436 cells covering barely half the slide, which is a scatter of very round cells
// rather than a smear of yeast. `marble.mm`'s own header has that measurement. Being nearly as
// round and twice as dense is the better picture, and it is why the wall stops at 200.
//
// Note that `soup.ron` names no inhabitants at all, which is why it falls back to the ancestor —
// see `seed_into` in `mm-app`. Naming them here is also how a saved slide remembers who was on
// it, so this is the ordinary way to author a population rather than a special case for a demo.
(
    name: "the marbles",
    seed: 20250728,
    width: 64,
    height: 64,

    light: Uniform(intensity: 1024),
    current: Still,
    fluid_interval: 1,

    // soup.ron's, unchanged. The world is the control; the genome is the experiment.
    seeding: [
        Uniform(chemical: 11, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 14, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 4, per_square: 409600),
    ],
    barriers: [],

    inhabitants: [
        (genome: "marble.mm", count: 16),
    ],

    // The other half of being a marble, and it only works as a pair with the genome.
    //
    // A firm cell is drawn at its true radius rather than 15% over it, so it crosses its
    // neighbours barely and the plane through the crossings barely cuts it. That takes a packed
    // sheet from 0.387 out of round — more angular than a square — to 0.167, between a hexagon and
    // a circle. **The rest of the distance is the cells not being pressed into one another**, and
    // that is not something a renderer can give: at a spacing where nothing physically touches the
    // same firmness reaches 0.088, rounder than a hexagon.
    //
    // `rigidity_gain` is what buys it. Wall times turgor scales `neighbours::core_permille`
    // towards `CORE_PERMILLE_RIGID`, which is a thousand — exactly tangency — so any overlap at
    // all is past the core and takes the stiff response. A firm cell cannot be that close to
    // another without pushing it away, which is what "marble" means mechanically.
    //
    // `separation_relax` is what makes that survivable. With the core at tangency every contact
    // takes the stiff branch, where it had been the rare deep-penetration case, and the
    // corrections are summed across a cell's neighbours — so a crowded cell is shoved several
    // times as far as any one contact asked for and the pack buzzes at 662 thousandths of a square
    // a tick. An eighth takes that to **7**, and leaves the pack better separated as well: 14% of
    // near pairs overlapping against 19%.
    //
    // Both are zero in `BiologyConfig::default`, for the reason `light_occlusion` is: they change
    // what a slide holds, and every acceptance result in the tree was taken without them.
    biology: (
        separation_relax: 128,
        metabolism: (
            rates: (
                rigidity_gain: 16384,
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
